        "original_id": "e0f08375-7be6-5348-bda9-caad018f0d0c",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Myrtus, Eustoma, myrtle, lisianthus, prairie gentian\n- Taxonomy: Myrtaceae, gentian family, flowering plants, genus\n- Concepts: fidelity, weddings, art, symbolism\n- People: Linnaeus\n\nFacts:\n- Linnaeus described the flowering plant genus Myrtus in 1753.\n- Myrtus, commonly called myrtle, is a genus of flowering plants in the family Myrtaceae.\n- From Roman times to the present, myrtle has been associated with fidelity and used in weddings.\n- In art, wreathed crowns of myrtle may be found as symbols connected to fidelity.\n- Eustoma, commonly known as lisianthus or prairie gentian, is a small genus in the gentian family.",
